Linux command line

Shell is a program that takes keyboard input
and passes it to the OS.

Shell prompt usually follows username@machinename
format.

date - prints current date.
cal - prints the calendar.
df - prints the current available space.
free - print currently free RAM.
pwd - print working directory.
ls - list files.
cd - change current directory, either using relative
or absolute path.

ls - list files; also can list mult. dirs.
ls -lt - long format plus sorted by time.

mkdir - create directory.
cp - copy file or a directory.
mv - move and rename.
rm - remove.
Better test the rm with echo or ls if using
globbing.

type - defines command's type.
which - determine location.
help & --help - display usage info.
man - display manual pages:
1. User commands
2. PI for kernel syscalls
3. PI for C libs
4. Special files such as device nodes and drivers
5. File formats
6. Games
7. Misc.
8. Sysadmin commands

apropos - display appropriate command.
whatis - display one-line manual page.
info - display command info entry.

/usr/share/doc - useful documentation for software.

alias - shortcut for defined series of commands.

> - redirect output; rewrites the file.
>> - append.
2> - redirect the stderr.
&> - redirect stdout and stderr to one file.

/dev/null - accepts input and does nothing;
used to supress output.

cat - display file content.
cat > - can join files.
| - redirect stdout to stdin.
sort - sort the input by pattern.
uniq - remove duplicates.
wc - print line, word and byte count.
grep - match pattern.
tail/head - print last/first n lines from file.
tail -f - watch the file in real time.
tee - read from stdin and output to stdout and files.
echo - print text to stdout.
printenv - list environment variables.
$() - command substitution; ex: ls -l $(which cp).

Escape sequences:
1. \a - bell
2. \b - backslash
3. \n - newline
4. \r - carriage return
5. \t - tab

id - display current identity.

/etc/passwd - user accounts.
/etc/group - group accounts.
/etc/shadow - user/group passwords.

umask - change standard rights for newly created files.

su - change current user.
sudo - execute command as (super)user.
chown - change file owner/group.

ps - display active processes.
ps x; ps aux;

Shell sessions stores environment.

Environment stores environment variables and
shell variables.

set | less - view envars sorted.
alias - display aliases.

/etc/profile - global cfg script that applies to all users.
~/.bash_profile - usr personal cfg file.
~/.bash_login - if previous is missing, bash tries to read this.
~/.profile - if previous two are missing, bash tries to read this.
/etc/bash.bashrc - global cfg script that applies to all users.
~/.bashrc - usr personal cfg file.

export - make envar available to the child processes.

$PS1 = terminal prompt.

/etc/fstab - lists devices that are to be mounted on boot.
mount - mount/display mounted devices.
umount - unmount the device.

Good practice is to create a separate directory for a mount point
in /mnt; like /mnt/cd_rom.

Linux storage devices names:
1. /dev/fd - Floppy disk drives.
2. /dev/hd - IDE (PATA) drives on older system.
3. /dev/lp - printers.
4. /dev/sd - SCSI disks; modern Linux treats all
disk-like devices as SCSI.
5. /dev/sr - Optical (CD/DVD) drives.

One way to look up the newly attached device's name is
look up the /var/log/messages or /var/log/syslog:
sudo tail -f /var/log/messages.

fdisk - manipulate partitions on a specified drive;
entering m will open a menu with options.
mkfs - make new file system.
fsck - file system check; repairs the damaged system.
dd - copy blocks of data from X to Y; it has special syntax:
dd if=input_file of=output_file [bs=block_size [count=blocks]]
Use with caution.

wodim - blank a rewritable CD-RW.
md5sum - generate checksum.

ping - send ICMP ECHO_REQUEST to a specified host.
traceroute - lists the "hops" from local system to specified host.
ip - multipurpose network config.

loopback interface - virtual system used to talk to itself.

netstat - examine network config and stats.
ftp - download files and connect to fileservers.
wget - download files.

locate - search filename in database.
